#!/bin/sh
# dd-ctl — the ONLY thing the destiny-director deploy key on `two` can do.
#
# THIS IS THE CANONICAL COPY, and the only one in the repo. It lives beside the stack it
# drives rather than in bin/, because it drives exactly this stack and nothing else;
# bin/ holds fleet-wide tools. There is no symlink to it anywhere, deliberately — a
# script reachable by two paths is a script whose behaviour can depend on which path you
# used. The README's "Run a stack" block points here so it is still findable.
#
# It carries no infra- header and is not under hosts/two/system/, so neither
# bin/check-system-drift nor bin/install-system-file sees it: those read the header as
# the single source of truth for tracked /etc copies (bin/_infra.py), and a header no
# parser reads would be decoration that looks like a checked invariant. The live copy is
# installed by hosts/two/setup/root-setup.sh §4. Drift is checked by hand:
#
#   md5sum /usr/local/bin/dd-ctl ~/infra/deployments/destiny-director/dd-ctl
#
# THAT md5sum IS NOT THE WHOLE DRIFT CHECK, and on its own it answers a narrower question
# than it appears to. sshd runs a forced command as `$SHELL -c "<command>"`, so four
# files execute as gavin BEFORE this script does, and none of them is dd-ctl:
#
#   ls -ln /etc/fish/config.fish ~/.profile ~/.config/fish/config.fish
#   ls -lnd ~ ~/.config ~/.config/fish ~/.config/fish/conf.d
#   ls -ln  ~/.config/fish/conf.d/            # expect podman.fish and nothing else
#
# A `md5sum` that matches while `~/.config/fish/conf.d/` has gained a second file is a
# green light for a dispatcher that no longer decides anything. root-setup.sh §4 runs
# exactly these checks and warns on anything it did not write; the lines above are how
# you ask the same question between runs of it.
#
# THE INSTALLED COPY MUST BE A REAL FILE OWNED BY ROOT, mode 0755, at
# /usr/local/bin/dd-ctl, in a directory only root can write — never a symlink into the
# checkout, and never the checkout's copy run in place. `gavin` owns ~/infra, so a
# forced command resolving through any path gavin can rewrite is not a restriction: the
# key holder replaces the target and has a shell. root-setup.sh §4 verifies all three
# properties after installing rather than trusting `install` to have produced them.
#
#   command="/usr/local/bin/dd-ctl",restrict ssh-ed25519 AAAA... dd-ctl@yourbox
#
# so `ssh two deploy-beacon` runs THIS with the client's string in
# SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND, and `ssh two` with no command runs it with that variable unset.
#
#   dd-ctl deploy-beacon | deploy-anchor | deploy-both | down | status
#          | logs-beacon | logs-anchor | logs-postgres
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE THREAT MODEL. Read the first paragraph before making any judgement about the rest.
#
# `gavin` IS IN `wheel` AND `sudo` IS INSTALLED. So anything that escapes this forced
# command lands on an account that can become root. There is nothing behind this script;
# it is the whole boundary, and its blast radius is root, not "some unprivileged user".
# Do not describe it anywhere as contained or unprivileged. It has NOT had a dedicated
# adversarial review — hosts/two/system/README.md lists what such a review still owes.
#
# SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND is attacker-controlled text: whoever holds the private half of
# that key writes it, and so does whoever steals the key. The design answer is to give
# that text almost nothing to act on.
#
# NO VERB TAKES AN ARGUMENT. That is the entire input-handling strategy, and it is a
# deliberate replacement for an earlier version of this file that accepted
# `deploy <bot> [tag|digest]` and `logs [service] [--tail N]`. That version carried tag
# charset validation, digest validation, `--tail` bounds checks, per-verb arity checks,
# a `..` rejection, and a controlled word-split under `set -f` with a pinned IFS —
# every line of which existed only to make arguments safe. Deleting the arguments
# deletes that entire class of bug, and the code that could be wrong about it.
#
# It also narrows a hole that no amount of validation would have: WITH AN ARGUMENT, THE
# KEYHOLDER CHOOSES THE IMAGE. `deploy beacon <tag>` let whoever held the key pick which
# tag was pulled, and therefore what code ran as a user with sudo — a perfectly
# well-formed tag pointing at an image of their choosing passes every charset check
# there is. With `deploy-beacon`, the image reference is a literal in compose.yaml,
# which only the operator can edit. Someone will eventually want to "improve" this by
# adding the tag back as an argument. That is the thing this file exists not to do.
#
# NARROWS, NOT CLOSES — be precise, because the weaker claim is the true one. The
# keyholder cannot choose which image or which branch. But compose.yaml names a MOVING
# BRANCH TAG, so anyone with push access to that branch decides what code runs on the
# next deploy, as a user in `wheel` with sudo. That trust boundary sits upstream of this
# box entirely and no amount of dispatcher design touches it. The lever, if it is ever
# wanted, is pointing compose.yaml at a branch only the owner pushes to, at the cost of
# a merge per deploy. Not done today. Written down so nobody reads the paragraph above
# as "locked down".
#
# What remains, therefore:
#
#   * The request is ONE string, compared against a closed list of exact literals and
#     then discarded. It is never word-split, never interpolated into a command, never
#     used as a path or a printf format, and never echoed back — echoing attacker text
#     into a terminal, or into a log somebody later `cat`s, hands them control sequences
#     for free. The audit log records the CATEGORY of a rejection, never the input.
#   * There is no eval, no `sh -c`, and no backticks. Every command below is a fixed
#     line of literals; nothing derived from input reaches podman in any form.
#   * `case` patterns are globs, so they are kept boringly literal. The hostile string
#     is always the SUBJECT and never a pattern, so it cannot introduce one.
#
# A stricter arrangement exists and was considered: one keypair per operation, each with
# its own fixed `command=` line in authorized_keys, so the dispatch happens inside
# sshd's key matching and this script handles no input at all. It was not chosen because
# it costs six keypairs to generate, install and hold. It is the fallback if the `case`
# below is ever judged insufficient.
#
# THE FORCED COMMAND IS NOT THE OUTERMOST THING THAT RUNS. sshd does not exec it — it
# runs `$SHELL -c "<command>"`, and `gavin`'s login shell is fish, which reads
# /etc/fish/config.fish, ~/.config/fish/conf.d/*.fish and ~/.config/fish/config.fish on
# every startup including a non-interactive `-c`. Those files are gavin-writable by
# construction and root-setup.sh §8 writes one of them, so the ambient login shell sits
# INSIDE this key's trusted computing base and no property of this file removes it.
# `restrict` does not help: it implies `no-user-rc`, which is about ~/.ssh/rc. What this
# script can do about it, and does, is refuse to inherit anything from that shell — see
# the `env -i` re-exec below. What it cannot do is stop the shell running first. The
# residual is recorded in docs/decisions.md and hosts/two/system/README.md rather than
# papered over, and root-setup.sh §4 checks those four paths' ownership and modes.
#
# `logs` AND `logs-postgres` HAND RAW APPLICATION OUTPUT TO A PRINCIPAL THIS FILE TREATS
# AS HOSTILE, and that is a deliberate asymmetry worth naming rather than discovering.
# `status` is filtered on purpose: it prints four named environment variables and never
# the container's whole environment, because .env holds Discord tokens and a Postgres
# password. The two log verbs have no such filter and cannot have a useful one — they
# exist to show what the bot printed, and the bot decides what it prints. So the deploy
# key can read whatever the bot logs, including anything it logs by mistake: a token in
# a traceback, a URL with a credential in it. That is accepted (the holder of this key
# can already deploy code as a user with sudo, so log contents are not the weak link),
# but the app's logging is part of what this key is trusted with, and nobody should be
# surprised by that later.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------
set -eu

# A forced command runs non-interactively: sshd sources neither /etc/profile nor
# ~/.profile, so nothing here may assume an environment. Set it all explicitly.
#
# NO /usr/local/sbin. Nothing this script runs lives there — podman, podman-compose,
# stat, date, free, df and mkdir are all in /usr/bin or /bin — and root-setup.sh §4
# establishes that ONE directory, /usr/local/bin, is root-owned and not group- or
# world-writable. Carrying a second local directory on PATH that nothing verifies is an
# unchecked place for a `podman` to appear ahead of the real one.
PATH=/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin
export PATH
LC_ALL=C
export LC_ALL

# --- the environment boundary -------------------------------------------------------
#
# RE-EXEC UNDER `env -i`, KEEPING FOUR NAMES. Without this, what reaches podman depends
# on an sshd_config nobody in this repo has read: `AcceptEnv` decides which client-set
# variables sshd imports, `PermitUserEnvironment` decides whether ~/.ssh/environment is
# read, and — the one that is certainly live — fish runs before this script and can
# export anything it likes (see the header). "Probably nothing gets through" is not a
# boundary; this is.
#
# The variables that make it worth doing are not exotic:
#
#   COMPOSE_PROJECT_NAME       podman-compose 1.6.0 lets it OVERRIDE the `name:` in
#                              compose.yaml, so `down` would tear down a different
#                              project, and a deploy would create a second stack beside
#                              the running one on a 475 MB board.
#   CONTAINERS_CONF            names a config file that can set the OCI runtime binary
#                              and its arguments. That is arbitrary code execution
#                              shaped like a configuration option.
#   CONTAINERS_STORAGE_CONF    the same, for storage: driver, graphroot, and mount
#                              programs that are executables it names.
#
# KEPT: HOME (podman's storage lives under it, and the audit log), USER (podman and
# podman-compose both read it in places), SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND (the request itself —
# hostile text, and treated as such below), and DD_CTL_REEXEC as the loop sentinel.
# SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND is passed even when unset, arriving as empty; the dispatch reads
# it with `${SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND-}` and an empty request is a usage error either way.
#
# `exec`, so there is no parent process left holding the discarded environment, and
# /usr/bin/env by absolute path so this does not depend on the PATH it is about to
# replace. The re-exec runs `$0`, which under the forced command is the root-owned
# /usr/local/bin/dd-ctl named in authorized_keys.
if [ -z "${DD_CTL_REEXEC:-}" ]; then
	exec /usr/bin/env -i \
		DD_CTL_REEXEC=1 \
		HOME="${HOME:-}" \
		USER="${USER:-}" \
		SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND="${SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND-}" \
		/bin/sh "$0" "$@"
fi

# Rootless podman needs XDG_RUNTIME_DIR and will not invent one. /run/user/<uid> is the
# usual answer, but /run is root-owned and nothing on this box creates that directory at
# boot: there is no logind, and the local.d hook that used to do it was removed with the
# boot autostart it also carried. So use podman's own fallback location, which an
# unprivileged process can create for itself.
#
# IT MUST BE THE SAME VALUE EVERYWHERE. Containers started under one XDG_RUNTIME_DIR are
# invisible to podman running under another — you get an empty `podman ps` next to a
# running bot. root-setup.sh §8 exports this same path into gavin's shells for that
# reason; if you change it here, change it there.
#
# /tmp IS WORLD-WRITABLE AND THIS PATH IS PREDICTABLE, so another local uid can create
# it first. Podman checks ownership and mode and would refuse, so the exposure is denial
# of service rather than takeover — but a clear refusal beats podman's, and a runtime
# directory that is silently the wrong one is how you get an empty `podman ps` beside a
# running bot. Created with `mkdir -m 700` in one step (mkdir-then-chmod leaves a window
# at the default mode), then VERIFIED, because creating it says nothing about what was
# already there.
# NO APOSTROPHE IN THIS MESSAGE, and that is not fussiness. shellcheck's parser treats
# a `'` inside a `${var:?…}` word as opening a single-quoted string, and then MISPARSES
# THE ENTIRE REST OF THE FILE — every check after this line becomes a cascade of bogus
# quoting errors, and the real findings are buried in them. That is how this file went
# its whole life having never actually been checked: `shellcheck -s sh dd-ctl` exited 0
# with nothing but quote noise. Keep the wording apostrophe-free so the checker works.
: "${HOME:?dd-ctl: HOME is unset — cannot locate the podman storage directory}"
XDG_RUNTIME_DIR="/tmp/podman-run-$(id -u)"
export XDG_RUNTIME_DIR
# shellcheck disable=SC2174  # -m applies to the deepest dir only; that is the only one
mkdir -m 700 -p "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" 2>/dev/null || :
if [ -L "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" ] || [ ! -d "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" ]; then
	printf 'dd-ctl: %s is missing, or is a symlink. Refusing.\n' "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" >&2
	exit 1
fi
_rt="$(stat -c '%u %a' "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR")"
if [ "$_rt" != "$(id -u) 700" ]; then
	printf 'dd-ctl: %s is "%s", want "%s 700".\n' "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR" "$_rt" "$(id -u)" >&2
	printf 'Another uid owns this runtime directory. Refusing to use it.\n' >&2
	exit 1
fi
unset _rt

# --- fixed configuration ----------------------------------------------------------
# Edit these if the checkout moves. They are literals, not templated at install time:
# the bytes reviewed are the bytes installed.
#
# NOT derived from $0. The installed copy lives at /usr/local/bin/dd-ctl, outside the
# checkout and root-owned (see the header), so there is no sibling compose.yaml for a
# $0-relative lookup to find. Stating the path outright is also the safer half of the
# trade: a resolved path is a path that whoever can move or link the script gets a say
# in, and this script's whole job is letting input decide nothing.
STACK_DIR=/home/gavin/infra/deployments/destiny-director
COMPOSE_FILE="$STACK_DIR/compose.yaml"

# THE IMAGE IS NOT CHOSEN HERE, and not by the caller. It is a literal in compose.yaml,
# editable only by the operator. See the header for why that is the point rather than an
# inconvenience.
#
# BOTH BOTS RUN AT ONCE, which is a change from this script's original design. There used
# to be one `BOT_CONTAINER=dd-bot`, and every deploy stopped and removed it before
# creating the next, so beacon and anchor could never be on the test guild together. That
# is no longer the rule: compose.yaml declares a service per bot, each deploy touches
# ONLY its own container, and `deploy-both` brings up the pair. Anything below that still
# reads as if there were one bot is a bug, not a survival.
BEACON_CONTAINER=dd-beacon
ANCHOR_CONTAINER=dd-anchor
DB_CONTAINER=dd-postgres

# jemalloc settings used to live here as MALLOC_BEACON/MALLOC_ANCHOR, exported per deploy
# into `${DD_MALLOC_CONF:?}`. They are now literals in compose.yaml, one per service —
# with a service per bot the value no longer has to be chosen at deploy time by anything.

TAIL_LINES=200    # fixed. The log verbs take no arguments; there is one per container.
STOP_TIMEOUT=60   # > supervisord's stopwaitsecs=45, so the bot gets its drain

# How long to wait for a bot to report itself online, and how often to look. The marker
# is hikari's own "started successfully in approx Ns" line — see wait_ready().
#
# 900s is not padding. A measured cold start on this board, single bot, with the image
# already pulled: container spawned 21:03:41, `started successfully` at 21:09:23 — 5m42s,
# of which ~4 minutes was Python import and extension loading before hikari printed
# anything at all. `deploy-both` runs two of those on one 700 MHz core, and they overlap.
# A timeout that expires on a healthy-but-slow start would be worse than no timeout: it
# reports failure for a bot that is about to come up, and whoever is reading it is
# reading it over SSH with no other context.
READY_TIMEOUT=900
READY_POLL=10

AUDIT_LOG="$HOME/.local/state/dd-ctl/audit.log"

# There is no state file. An earlier version recorded the last deploy so `down` and
# `status` could reconstruct it, and then re-validated that file on read because a file
# we wrote is still a file that can be edited. `status` now asks the RUNNING CONTAINER
# which bot it is, which is both the truthful answer and one less thing to parse.

die() { printf 'dd-ctl: %s\n' "$1" >&2; exit "${2:-1}"; }

# WORK FROM THE STACK DIRECTORY, because podman-compose 1.6.0 — the version Alpine 3.24
# ships and this box runs — reads a `.env` from the CURRENT WORKING DIRECTORY as well as
# the one beside the compose file, and lets the CWD copy win. Under the forced command
# the cwd is $HOME, so `~/.env` would silently override the stack's — a file gavin can
# create by accident and a keyholder could not, but which decides POSTGRES_PASSWORD and
# DATABASE_URL either way. Proven on 1.6.0, not inferred.
#
# `cd` rather than `--env-file "$STACK_DIR/.env"`: cd fixes the whole class (any relative
# path podman-compose resolves against the cwd), whereas --env-file fixes exactly the one
# instance of it and adds a flag whose meaning has already moved once between the two
# versions this box might see. Nothing else here depends on the cwd — COMPOSE_FILE is
# absolute, and podman-compose realpaths the compose file's own directory for `env_file:`
# and relative volume sources regardless of where it was started from.
cd -- "$STACK_DIR" || die "cannot cd to $STACK_DIR — is the checkout there?"

# --- helpers ----------------------------------------------------------------------
usage() {
	cat >&2 <<'EOF'
dd-ctl — restricted deploy control for the destiny-director test stack on `two`

Eight operations. NONE of them takes an argument — that is deliberate, see the
comments at the top of this file.

  deploy-beacon    pull and (re)create dd.beacon. Leaves anchor alone.
  deploy-anchor    pull and (re)create dd.anchor. Leaves beacon alone.
  deploy-both      deploy beacon, wait until it is on the gateway, then anchor.
                   Waits again. Both bots end up running.
  down             stop and remove the stack. Named volumes are NEVER removed.
  status           what is running, what each bot thinks it is, and the
                   runtime's own view of itself
  logs-beacon      last 200 lines from beacon
  logs-anchor      last 200 lines from anchor
  logs-postgres    last 200 lines from postgres

BOTH BOTS RUN AT ONCE on this box. `logs` is gone — there are two bots now, and
no verb here takes an argument, so there is a verb per container instead.

Which image is deployed is set in compose.yaml, by the operator, on the box.
There is no follow mode: a forced command that never returns is a hung session
— `deploy-both` is the one long-running verb, and it is bounded (see
READY_TIMEOUT).
EOF
	exit 2
}

audit() {  # only ever called with a literal this script chose — never with input
	mkdir -p "$(dirname -- "$AUDIT_LOG")"
	printf '%s %s\n' "$(date -u '+%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ')" "$1" >> "$AUDIT_LOG"
}

# --- one at a time ------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# NON-BLOCKING, AND ONLY AROUND THE MUTATING VERBS. `two` is a 475 MB board with NO
# cgroup delegation (so no per-container memory limit is possible) and, after a reboot,
# NO swap. Four to six concurrent deploys — trivial to trigger, since every one of them
# is one `ssh two deploy-beacon` and the deploy takes minutes on this CPU — each pull a
# layer set, each start a postgres, and the board runs out of memory. The plausible OOM
# victim is not the bot: it is `cloudflared`, which is the only way into this box. A
# deploy storm that costs you the box is a worse outcome than a deploy that is refused.
#
# `-n`, never a wait. A forced command that blocks is a hung SSH session, and a queue of
# them is the same memory problem arriving more slowly.
#
# `status`, `logs` and `logs-postgres` DO NOT TAKE THE LOCK, deliberately. They are
# read-only and cheap, and the moment you most want to look at the logs is while a deploy
# is running or stuck — locking those out would remove the only diagnostic the keyholder
# has, to prevent nothing.
#
# busybox's flock takes a file descriptor as its argument (`flock -n 9`), which is what
# this uses; the lock is released when the process exits and the fd closes. If flock is
# somehow absent this REFUSES rather than continuing unlocked: a guard that silently
# is not there is worse than no guard, because the header above now says there is one.
#
# THE fd MUST BE CLOSED FOR EVERY podman CHILD — see `pod()` and `compose()` below, which
# both append `9>&-`. An open fd is inherited across fork+exec, and podman leaves
# LONG-LIVED supervisors behind: one `conmon` per container plus `aardvark-dns`, all of
# which outlive the dd-ctl that started them and run for as long as the stack is up.
# Without `9>&-` they inherit this lock and hold it forever, so the FIRST successful
# deploy locks out every deploy after it. Observed on `two` exactly that way:
#
#     pid 9400 (aardvark-dns) holds 9 -> /tmp/podman-run-1000/dd-ctl.lock
#     pid 9403 (conmon)       holds 9 -> ...   [dd-postgres]
#     pid 9505 (conmon)       holds 9 -> ...   [dd-bot]
#
# and every subsequent `deploy-beacon` refused with "another dd-ctl is already running"
# while nothing was running. Note the failure is not a hang or a crash — it is a guard
# that reports a true-sounding thing that is false, which is the worst shape available.
LOCK_FILE="$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR/dd-ctl.lock"
take_lock() {
	command -v flock >/dev/null 2>&1 \
		|| die "flock is not installed, so the single-flight lock cannot be taken. Refusing."
	exec 9>"$LOCK_FILE"
	flock -n 9 || die "another dd-ctl is already running (a deploy or a down). Nothing was
        started. \`status\`, \`logs\` and \`logs-postgres\` still work while it runs.
        If nothing IS running, a previous deploy's conmon may be holding the lock fd;
        check with: for p in /proc/[0-9]*; do readlink \$p/fd/9 2>/dev/null | grep -q
        dd-ctl.lock && echo \$p; done"
}

# Every podman invocation goes through one of these two, so the `9>&-` that stops the
# lock fd leaking into podman's long-lived children lives in exactly two places rather
# than being repeated at each call site (where the next one added would forget it).
pod() { podman "$@" 9>&-; }

# podman-compose HAS FLIPPED .env-vs-process-environment precedence between releases:
#
#   1.0.6   self.environ = dict(os.environ) ; .update(dotenv_dict)   -> .env WINS
#   1.6.0   self.environ = dotenv_dict      ; .update(os.environ)    -> process env WINS
#
# Alpine 3.24 ships 1.6.0. This USED to be load-bearing: compose.yaml interpolated
# `${DD_BOT:?}`, this script exported it, and under 1.0.6 a stray uncommented DD_BOT in
# .env would have pinned every deploy to one bot while this script cheerfully reported
# the other. compose.yaml now names both bots as literals in a service each, so .env
# cannot win that argument no matter which version is installed, and DD_BOT/DD_MALLOC_CONF
# are not read by anything any more.
#
# THE CHECK STAYS ANYWAY, for the two names that still mean something. RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME
# in .env would be shadowed by `environment:` today — but "shadowed by a precedence rule
# that has already flipped once" is exactly the kind of thing this file does not rely on,
# and a RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME sitting in .env is a misconception worth failing on rather
# than silently ignoring. DD_BOT and DD_MALLOC_CONF are kept in the list for one release
# so that an .env left over from the one-bot design is reported instead of quietly
# ignored; they can go once no such .env exists.
assert_env_clean() {
	[ -f "$STACK_DIR/.env" ] || die "no $STACK_DIR/.env — copy .env.example and fill it in"
	for var in DD_BOT DD_MALLOC_CONF RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME MALLOC_CONF; do
		if grep -Eq "^[[:space:]]*(export[[:space:]]+)?${var}[[:space:]]*=" "$STACK_DIR/.env"; then
			die "$var is set in $STACK_DIR/.env — compose.yaml supplies it, per service.
        Comment it out. (If this is DD_BOT or DD_MALLOC_CONF, it is a leftover from the
        one-bot-at-a-time design and is simply dead now.)"
		fi
	done
}

compose() { podman-compose -f "$COMPOSE_FILE" "$@" 9>&-; }

# --- named volumes only, enforced twice ---------------------------------------------
#
# THE QUESTION THIS ANSWERS is the third row of the table in hosts/two/system/README.md:
# can the deployed stack gain a writable mount under $HOME? A bind mount over ~/.ssh
# would let the keyholder rewrite their own authorized_keys line — strip `restrict` and
# the `command=` — and walk out of the forced command entirely. It does not need a
# container escape and it does not need root: rootless podman maps container uid 0 to
# gavin's real uid, so anything the container writes into a bind-mounted host path is
# written AS GAVIN. `compose.yaml` uses only named volumes today, but until this existed
# that was a property of the file, not an invariant, and nothing checked it on deploy.
#
# TWO LAYERS, because they fail differently:
#
#   1. `assert_compose_named_volumes_only` reads compose.yaml BEFORE anything is pulled
#      or stopped. It cannot destroy anything; the worst it can do is refuse a deploy
#      and print the line it objected to, with the file right there to read. It is a
#      text scan, not a YAML parser, and it is written to over-refuse rather than
#      under-refuse: any volume list item whose source is not a bare name is rejected.
#   2. `assert_no_bind_mounts` asks PODMAN, after the container exists, and catches
#      anything that arrives by a route the text scan does not model — long-form
#      `type: bind`, a mount podman-compose synthesises, a future compose feature. A
#      container that fails it is stopped and removed before this exits, because by then
#      the mount is live and refusing without removing it would be a warning, not a
#      guard.
#
# LAYER 2 IS NOW CALIBRATED, against a running dd-bot on 2026-08-06, which is what
# CLAUDE.md's rule asks for before a new check's verdict is trusted. It rests on podman's
# `.Mounts` listing only user-requested mounts (not /etc/resolv.conf, /etc/hosts,
# /run/.containerenv, which podman reports elsewhere), and that held:
#
#     podman inspect --format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Type}}|{{.Name}}|{{.Destination}}
#     {{end}}' dd-bot
#     volume|destiny-director_sshhostkeys|/home/dd/.ssh-host
#
# Exactly one line, and no bind mount among podman's own injected files. Each bot
# container should now show exactly one such line for ITS OWN host-key volume
# (sshhostkeys_beacon / sshhostkeys_anchor). Anything else and this check is wrong, not
# the box.
assert_compose_named_volumes_only() {
	awk '
		{ l = $0; sub(/#.*/, "", l); sub(/[[:space:]]+$/, "", l) }
		l == "" { next }
		{ match(l, /^[[:space:]]*/); ind = RLENGTH }
		l ~ /^[[:space:]]*volumes:[[:space:]]*$/ { in_vol = 1; vol_ind = ind; next }
		in_vol && l ~ /^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]/ {
			item = l
			sub(/^[[:space:]]*-[[:space:]]+/, "", item)
			gsub(/^["'"'"']|["'"'"']$/, "", item)
			if (item ~ /^type:[[:space:]]*bind/) {
				printf "  long-form bind mount: %s\n", item; bad++; next
			}
			src = item; sub(/:.*/, "", src)
			# A named volume source is a BARE NAME. No slash, no dot, no tilde, no
			# dollar: each of those is either a host path already or an interpolation
			# that could become one. Dots are excluded even though podman would accept
			# a volume called `a.b`, because `.` and `..` are the shapes that matter and
			# over-refusing here costs one rename.
			if (src !~ /^[A-Za-z0-9][A-Za-z0-9_-]*$/) {
				printf "  host-path volume: %s\n", item; bad++
			}
			next
		}
		in_vol && ind <= vol_ind { in_vol = 0 }
		l ~ /^[[:space:]]*source:[[:space:]]*[\/~.$]/ {
			printf "  long-form bind source: %s\n", l; bad++
		}
		END { exit (bad > 0) }
	' "$COMPOSE_FILE" || die "$COMPOSE_FILE declares a host-path volume (listed above).
        Refusing to deploy. A bind mount under \$HOME lets whatever runs in the
        container write host files AS GAVIN — ~/.ssh/authorized_keys included, which
        is the file that restricts the key that triggered this deploy. Named volumes
        only. If a host path is genuinely wanted, that is a decision for whoever owns
        this box, made in compose.yaml and in this check, not worked around."
}

assert_no_bind_mounts() {  # assert_no_bind_mounts <container>
	_mounts="$(pod inspect \
		--format '{{range .Mounts}}{{.Type}}|{{.Source}}|{{.Destination}}{{println}}{{end}}' \
		"$1" 2>/dev/null)" \
		|| die "cannot inspect $1's mounts, so whether it has a bind mount is unknown.
        Not proceeding on an unanswered question."
	if printf '%s\n' "$_mounts" | grep -q '^bind|'; then
		printf 'dd-ctl: %s has a BIND MOUNT. Its mounts:\n' "$1" >&2
		printf '%s\n' "$_mounts" | sed 's/^/        /' >&2
		pod stop --ignore --time 10 "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
		pod rm --ignore --force "$1" >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
		die "$1 has been STOPPED AND REMOVED. A bind mount writes host files as gavin,
        so a mount under \$HOME can rewrite ~/.ssh/authorized_keys — the file that
        restricts the key that asked for this deploy. Named volumes only."
	fi
}

# MIGRATION GUARD, and a dangerous one to omit. compose.yaml used to declare a single
# service `bot` with container_name `dd-bot`; it now declares `beacon` and `anchor`. A
# `dd-bot` left over from before the rename is INVISIBLE to podman-compose — the project
# no longer has a service that owns it — so `down` will not stop it and `up` will not
# replace it. It just keeps running, under `restart: always`, as a second copy of
# whichever bot it was, on the same test guild as the new one. That is precisely the
# duplicate this stack has always been arranged to prevent, arriving by the one route
# nothing was watching.
#
# It is removed rather than merely refused, because there is no reading under which a
# `dd-bot` should still exist: no compose file in this repo can create one any more.
#
# THIS IS DELETABLE once no box has a pre-rename container left — it is a one-time
# migration, not an invariant. Left in with its own name and this comment so that
# deleting it later is an obvious call rather than an archaeological one.
assert_no_legacy_bot() {
	pod container exists dd-bot 2>/dev/null || return 0
	# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # the backticks are prose and are meant to stay literal
	printf 'dd-ctl: found a legacy `dd-bot` container from the one-bot-at-a-time\n' >&2
	printf '        layout. compose.yaml no longer owns it, so nothing else would ever\n' >&2
	printf '        stop it and it would run as a duplicate bot. Removing it.\n' >&2
	pod stop --ignore --time "$STOP_TIMEOUT" dd-bot >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
	pod rm --ignore --force dd-bot >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
	# `if`, NOT `pod container exists … && die`. `set -e` is on, and an && list whose
	# left side fails makes the LIST fail — so the happy path here (the container is
	# gone, `exists` returns non-zero) would have exited the script with status 1 from
	# the one branch that means everything worked.
	if pod container exists dd-bot 2>/dev/null; then
		die "could not remove the legacy dd-bot container. Refusing to deploy on top
        of it — that would put two of the same bot on the test guild. Remove it by hand
        with 'podman rm -f dd-bot' and try again."
	fi
	return 0
}

# Blocks until a bot reports itself online, or gives up with a message that says what to
# look at. Called only with a literal container name this script chose.
#
# THE SIGNAL IS A LOG LINE, and that deserves justifying rather than apologising for.
# The obvious alternative — a container healthcheck — does not exist on this box: podman
# schedules healthchecks with systemd transient timers, there is no systemd here, and a
# healthcheck compiles to something that never runs (see compose.yaml, where one was
# deliberately not added). `podman ps` reporting Up is no answer either: Up means
# supervisord is alive, and supervisord is alive for the whole ~4 minutes of Python
# imports before hikari has done anything, and stays alive if the bot then crash-loops.
# The only thing on this box that knows the bot is on Discord is the bot, and the only
# way it says so is this line.
#
# NO STALE-MATCH RISK, because the container this greps was just created by
# --force-recreate: a new container has a new, empty log. It is NOT safe to reuse this
# against a container that has been up for a while.
wait_ready() {  # wait_ready <container> <bot>
	_c=$1; _b=$2; _waited=0
	printf '==> waiting for %s to come online (up to %ss)\n' "$_b" "$READY_TIMEOUT"
	while :; do
		# hikari's own line, printed once the shard has identified and the bot is on the
		# gateway. Matched as a fixed string, not a regex with a number in it.
		if pod logs "$_c" 2>&1 | grep -qF 'started successfully in approx'; then
			printf '==> %s is online after %ss\n' "$_b" "$_waited"
			return 0
		fi
		# Fail FAST rather than waiting out the timeout when the container is gone or
		# stopped. `restart: always` means a crashing bot comes back and this keeps
		# waiting — which is correct, since a slow crash-loop is indistinguishable from a
		# slow start until the timeout says otherwise — but a container that no longer
		# exists is never going to print anything.
		_state=$(pod inspect --format '{{.State.Status}}' "$_c" 2>/dev/null || true)
		case "$_state" in
			running|restarting|created|"") : ;;
			*) die "$_b's container is '$_state', not running. It will not come online.
        Look at 'dd-ctl logs-$_b'." ;;
		esac
		[ "$_waited" -lt "$READY_TIMEOUT" ] || die "$_b did not come online within
        ${READY_TIMEOUT}s. It may still be starting — this board is slow and the timeout
        is a guess, not a verdict — or it may be crash-looping under 'restart: always',
        which looks identical from here. The container was NOT touched; it is still
        running and still trying. Look at 'dd-ctl logs-$_b'."
		sleep "$READY_POLL"
		_waited=$((_waited + READY_POLL))
	done
}

deploy() {  # deploy <beacon|anchor> — called only with a literal
	bot=$1
	case "$bot" in
		beacon) container=$BEACON_CONTAINER ;;
		anchor) container=$ANCHOR_CONTAINER ;;
		*) die "internal: unknown bot" 1 ;;
	esac
	assert_env_clean
	assert_compose_named_volumes_only
	assert_no_legacy_bot

	# 1. PULL FIRST, EXPLICITLY, AND UNCONDITIONALLY. compose.yaml names a moving branch
	#    tag, and podman will not re-resolve a tag it already holds locally: without this
	#    step `up -d` happily keeps running last week's image under the same name. It is
	#    `podman-compose pull` rather than `podman pull <ref>` so the image reference
	#    stays in exactly one place — duplicating it here is how the two drift.
	#
	#    NO DIGEST COMPARISON, NO CHANGE DETECTION, deliberately: the deploy is a
	#    deliberate act somebody triggered over SSH, so "pull and recreate" is what they
	#    asked for. Skipping work when the digest looks unchanged is not an optimisation
	#    here, it is a second thing that can be wrong about what is running.
	#
	#    Doing it as its own step also means a registry problem is reported as a registry
	#    problem. The most likely one by far: GHCR packages are PRIVATE by default and
	#    visibility is a setting on the package in GitHub's UI, not something the
	#    workflow can grant — an unauthenticated pull from this box 403s until an owner
	#    flips it.
	printf '==> pulling the images named in compose.yaml\n'
	if ! compose pull; then
		cat >&2 <<'EOF'
dd-ctl: pull failed.
        If this is a 403/denied: the GHCR package is probably still private.
        Package page -> Package settings -> Danger Zone -> Change visibility.
EOF
		exit 1
	fi
	#    …AND DO NOT TRUST THAT EXIT CODE ON ITS OWN. podman-compose 1.0.6 runs the pull
	#    in a way that DISCARDS podman's exit status — proven: a pull of a nonexistent
	#    tag printed the error and returned 0. 1.6.0 propagates it, but "this version
	#    propagates it" is a fact about a version, and the box's podman-compose is
	#    upgraded by `apk upgrade` like everything else. Step 2 below destroys the
	#    running bot before step 3 creates one, so a pull that silently failed leaves no
	#    bot at all and a message that said nothing was wrong.
	#
	#    So assert the POSTCONDITION instead of the exit code: every image named in
	#    compose.yaml is now in local storage. That is version-independent, and it reads
	#    the references out of compose.yaml rather than restating them here — the one
	#    place they are allowed to live (see the header, and compose.yaml's own).
	compose_images="$(awk '$1 == "image:" && NF >= 2 { print $2 }' "$COMPOSE_FILE")"
	[ -n "$compose_images" ] || die "no image: line found in $COMPOSE_FILE. Refusing —
        this script cannot confirm what the pull was supposed to fetch."
	#    THIS RUNS BEFORE STEP 2 ON PURPOSE, so its failure direction is the safe one: a
	#    refusal here leaves the running bot exactly where it was. That matters because
	#    one input to it is unverified from anywhere but the box — whether `podman image
	#    exists` matches a `name:tag@sha256:…` reference (postgres is pinned that way).
	#    If it does not, the symptom is this refusal on a box that is actually fine; drop
	#    the tag from the pin in compose.yaml and it becomes a plain digest reference.
	#    A check that refuses wrongly is a nuisance; one that destroys a bot wrongly is
	#    not, which is why the order is this way round.
	for img in $compose_images; do
		pod image exists "$img" || die "the pull reported success but $img is not in
        local storage. Nothing has been stopped. Do not read this as a transient
        failure: on podman-compose 1.0.6 a failed pull exits 0, so an exit code is not
        evidence here. Check the pull output above."
	done

	# 2. Stop and remove THIS BOT'S container — and only this bot's. Under the old
	#    one-bot-at-a-time design this step was what stopped beacon and anchor ever
	#    coexisting; that is no longer its job, and it must not touch the other bot.
	#
	#    It still earns its place: --force-recreate below removes the container with
	#    podman's own default stop timeout, whereas this stops it with $STOP_TIMEOUT,
	#    which is deliberately longer than supervisord's stopwaitsecs=45 so the bot
	#    actually gets its drain instead of being killed mid-flush. --ignore makes it a
	#    no-op when nothing is there, so it is safe on a first deploy.
	pod stop --ignore --time "$STOP_TIMEOUT" "$container" >/dev/null
	pod rm --ignore --force "$container" >/dev/null

	# 3. Database first (a no-op when already up), then the bot, force-recreated so a
	#    moving tag actually takes effect — `up -d` alone will happily keep a running
	#    container whose tag now points at a different image.
	#
	#    STEP 2 HAS ALREADY REMOVED THIS BOT, so a failure anywhere in this step leaves
	#    the box without it — not with the previous copy of it. The OTHER bot, if it was
	#    running, is untouched throughout. Whoever reads the failure will be reading it
	#    over SSH with no other context, so each failure path says outright which bots
	#    are down rather than leaving them to infer it.
	printf '==> bringing up postgres\n'
	compose up -d postgres || die "postgres did not come up.
        $bot IS NOT RUNNING: step 2 removed its container before this step, and this step
        never got as far as creating a new one. Any OTHER bot is unaffected. Look at
        'dd-ctl logs-postgres' and 'dd-ctl status', fix the cause, and run the deploy
        again — it is idempotent."
	printf '==> creating the bot (%s)\n' "$bot"
	#    --no-deps IS LOAD-BEARING AND COST A DATABASE. Each bot service declares
	#    `depends_on: postgres`, and podman-compose 1.6.0 applies --force-recreate to EVERY
	#    container
	#    it brings up — dependencies included. So without this flag the line below
	#    destroys and recreates dd-postgres, roughly a minute after the previous line
	#    started it. Proven on the box, same compose file: `up -d --force-recreate <bot>`
	#    changed dd-postgres's container ID.
	#
	#    Against an existing database that is a pointless bounce. ON A FRESH pgdata
	#    VOLUME IT IS FATAL, because `initdb` on this board takes longer than that gap.
	#    The entrypoint appends the `host all all all …` line to pg_hba.conf and creates
	#    POSTGRES_DB only AFTER initdb returns, so a recreate landing mid-initdb leaves a
	#    data directory that has PG_VERSION but neither. Every subsequent start then
	#    prints "Skipping initialization" and the cluster is stuck FOREVER — observed
	#    twice, with only `template1` in pg_database (no template0, no postgres, no
	#    kyber) and a pg_hba.conf carrying no non-localhost entry. The bot loops on
	#    "Database unavailable" against a server that is up, healthy, and will never be
	#    usable. The only repair is `podman volume rm destiny-director_pgdata` and a
	#    redeploy, which is why this is a comment and not a one-word flag.
	#
	#    postgres is already up from the previous step, so skipping deps is also just
	#    correct: deploying a bot has no business touching the database — nor, now that
	#    there are two of them, the other bot.
	#
	#    THE SERVICE NAME IS THE BOT NAME. compose.yaml's services are `beacon` and
	#    `anchor` (they were one service called `bot`), so $bot names the service here and
	#    the container name is derived separately above. They must not be confused: the
	#    service is what podman-compose is told, the container is what podman is asked
	#    about afterwards.
	compose up -d --no-deps --force-recreate "$bot" || die "the $bot container was not created.
        $bot IS NOT RUNNING: step 2 removed its container before this step. postgres is
        up, $bot is absent, and any OTHER bot is unaffected. Run 'dd-ctl status', fix the
        cause, and run the deploy again."

	#    And before anything else looks at it: no bind mounts. This is layer 2 of the
	#    named-volumes-only rule, and it is here rather than after the verification below
	#    because a bind mount under $HOME is live from the moment the container starts.
	assert_no_bind_mounts "$container"
	assert_no_bind_mounts "$DB_CONTAINER"

	# 4. Verify END TO END, from the container that actually exists, that it is running
	#    the bot that was asked for. Everything upstream of this is a proxy, and every
	#    one of those proxies has moved at least once: podman-compose flipped .env vs
	#    process-env precedence between 1.0.6 and 1.6.0, and 1.6.0 stopped passing
	#    `env_file:` as `--env-file` and now expands it into `-e` pairs instead. Both
	#    changes leave `environment:` winning — but "still winning" is a fact to check,
	#    not to assume. This asks the only question that matters, of the only thing that
	#    can answer it.
	sleep 2
	bot_env=$(pod inspect --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' \
		"$container" 2>/dev/null || true)
	if printf '%s\n' "$bot_env" | grep -qx "RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME=$bot"; then
		printf '==> verified: %s is running as %s\n' "$container" "$bot"
	else
		cat >&2 <<EOF
dd-ctl: FAILED VERIFICATION. $container does not carry RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME=$bot.
        The container may be running the OTHER bot, or none at all. Most likely cause:
        RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME is set in $STACK_DIR/.env and is winning, or this
        podman-compose version resolves env precedence differently. Do not assume the
        deploy worked. Check:
            dd-ctl status
EOF
		exit 1
	fi
	seen_malloc=$(printf '%s\n' "$bot_env" | sed -n 's/^MALLOC_CONF=//p' | head -n1)
	if [ -n "$seen_malloc" ]; then
		printf '==> MALLOC_CONF: %s\n' "$seen_malloc"
	else
		printf 'dd-ctl: warning — no MALLOC_CONF in the container. Costs anchor a little RAM.\n' >&2
	fi
	printf '==> created. Startup on this CPU is slow — a measured cold start was 5m42s.\n'
}

status() {
	printf '=== containers ===\n'
	pod ps -a \
		--filter "name=$BEACON_CONTAINER" \
		--filter "name=$ANCHOR_CONTAINER" \
		--filter "name=$DB_CONTAINER" \
		--format 'table {{.Names}}\t{{.Status}}\t{{.Image}}' || true
	# Per bot, and BOTH are reported whether or not both are deployed — "(not deployed)"
	# is an answer, and a status that silently omitted the missing one would read as if
	# only one had ever been asked for.
	for _pair in "beacon $BEACON_CONTAINER" "anchor $ANCHOR_CONTAINER"; do
		# shellcheck disable=SC2086  # deliberate split of a literal pair this loop wrote
		set -- $_pair
		printf '\n=== %s: what the container actually thinks it is ===\n' "$1"
		# Asked of the container, not read back out of a file this script wrote. It is
		# the only source that cannot agree with a mistake made earlier in the pipeline.
		pod inspect --format '{{range .Config.Env}}{{println .}}{{end}}' "$2" 2>/dev/null \
			| grep -E '^(RAILWAY_SERVICE_NAME|MALLOC_CONF|DATABASE_SSL|TEST_ENV)=' \
			|| printf '(not deployed)\n'
		# Leading '%s' rather than a literal '---': a format string that STARTS with a
		# dash is a printf option as far as POSIX is concerned (shellcheck SC3045).
		printf '%s\n' "--- $1 image ---"
		# Both bots come from ONE image and are expected to be identical here. If these
		# two blocks ever disagree, someone edited one service's `image:` and not the
		# other, and that is worth seeing rather than averaging away.
		pod inspect --format '{{.ImageName}}{{"\n"}}{{.Image}}' "$2" 2>/dev/null \
			|| printf '(not deployed)\n'
	done
	printf '\n=== runtime ===\n'
	# The four things worth confirming rather than assuming on this board: native
	# rootless overlay (not vfs, which copies every layer onto the SD card), crun,
	# cgroup v2, and netavark — see root-setup.sh §2 and §6.
	pod info --format \
		'storage    {{.Store.GraphDriverName}}
runtime    {{.Host.OCIRuntime.Name}}
cgroups    {{.Host.CgroupsVersion}} / manager {{.Host.CgroupManager}}
network    {{.Host.NetworkBackend}}
rootless   {{.Host.Security.Rootless}}' 2>/dev/null || printf '(pod info failed)\n'
	printf 'compose    %s\n' "$(podman-compose --version 2>&1 | sed -n 's/^podman-compose version //p' | head -n1)"
	printf 'runtimedir %s\n' "$XDG_RUNTIME_DIR"
	printf '\n=== memory and disk ===\n'
	# RAM was the only resource printed here, and it is the one that bites first. But
	# this stack's other cost is a slow leak, not a spike: every deploy pulls a new image
	# under a MOVING TAG, which leaves the previous one behind as an untagged image on a
	# 29 G SD card that also holds the Postgres data directory. Nothing prunes it, and
	# nothing here will: `podman image prune -f` after a verified deploy was considered
	# and rejected, because the previous image is the only thing on this box you can roll
	# back to, and reclaiming it automatically would delete that on the very deploys that
	# are most likely to need it. Measure before deleting (CLAUDE.md) — so `status`
	# reports the number and a human decides. `podman image prune` by hand is one line.
	free -m || true
	printf '\n'
	df -h / || true
	printf '\n=== podman storage ===\n'
	pod system df 2>/dev/null || printf '(pod system df failed)\n'
	printf '\nNothing on this box starts at boot. After a reboot or a power cut the\n'
	# shellcheck disable=SC2016  # the backticks are prose, and are meant to stay literal
	printf 'stack stays down until someone runs `dd-ctl deploy-both`. That is intended.\n'
}

# --- dispatch ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# ONE string. When this runs as a forced command sshd hands the client's entire request
# over in SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND; when a human runs it on the box it is the first
# argument. Either way it is compared against fixed literals and then discarded — never
# split into words, never interpolated into a command, never used as a path, never
# logged. No verb takes an argument, so more than one is simply a rejection.
[ "$#" -le 1 ] || { audit "REJECTED too-many-arguments"; usage; }
if [ "$#" -eq 1 ]; then
	request="$1"
else
	request="${SSH_ORIGINAL_COMMAND-}"
fi

# The subject is quoted, so no word splitting. The patterns are plain literals with no
# glob metacharacters — `case` patterns ARE globs, and while the hostile string is
# always the subject and can never become a pattern, keeping them boring means nobody
# has to re-derive that.
#
# THE THREE MUTATING VERBS TAKE THE LOCK; the three read-only ones do not. See
# take_lock() for why, including why it refuses rather than waits.
case "$request" in
deploy-beacon)
	audit "deploy-beacon"
	take_lock
	deploy beacon
	;;
deploy-anchor)
	audit "deploy-anchor"
	take_lock
	deploy anchor
	;;
deploy-both)
	audit "deploy-both"
	# ONE LOCK FOR THE WHOLE SEQUENCE, taken once here rather than by each deploy. This
	# is a single mutating operation that happens to have two halves; a second caller
	# arriving between them would be exactly the concurrent-deploy case take_lock exists
	# to refuse.
	take_lock
	# SEQUENTIAL, AND THE WAIT BETWEEN THEM IS THE POINT — not politeness. One 700 MHz
	# core: starting the second bot while the first is still doing its ~4 minutes of
	# Python imports means both crawl, and on a 512 MB board they would also reach their
	# peak startup allocation at the same moment. Waiting for the first to be genuinely
	# on the gateway staggers the two costs that actually collide.
	#
	# BEACON FIRST, deliberately. It is the lighter of the two, so if the pair does not
	# fit in RAM the failure lands on anchor — the one being added — rather than taking
	# out a beacon that was fine on its own.
	deploy beacon
	wait_ready "$BEACON_CONTAINER" beacon
	deploy anchor
	wait_ready "$ANCHOR_CONTAINER" anchor
	printf '==> both bots are online.\n'
	;;
down)
	audit "down"
	take_lock
	# No --volumes, ever, and no way to ask for it. The pgdata volume is the only state
	# this box holds for the stack; losing it to a mistyped verb is not a recoverable
	# mistake, and `podman volume rm` by hand is the right amount of friction.
	compose down
	printf '==> stack down. The pgdata volume is untouched.\n'
	;;
status)
	audit "status"
	status
	;;
logs-beacon)
	audit "logs-beacon"
	# ONE VERB PER CONTAINER, not `logs <bot>`. There used to be a single `logs` for the
	# single bot; with two bots the alternative to two verbs is an argument, and no verb
	# here takes an argument — see the header. Two literals cost a line each.
	#
	# exec: no shell survives the dispatch. Raw `podman logs` against the known
	# container name rather than `podman-compose logs` — it needs no compose parsing,
	# so no interpolation, so no way for a missing variable to turn "show me the logs"
	# into an error, and its flags have been stable across every podman this box will
	# see. Every word of the command line is a literal or a constant set above.
	# `exec` replaces the shell with a BINARY, so it cannot run the pod() function —
	# hence podman directly here, with the same 9>&- the wrapper would have applied.
	exec podman logs --tail "$TAIL_LINES" "$BEACON_CONTAINER" 9>&-
	;;
logs-anchor)
	audit "logs-anchor"
	# Direct podman, not pod(): see the note on `logs-beacon` above.
	exec podman logs --tail "$TAIL_LINES" "$ANCHOR_CONTAINER" 9>&-
	;;
logs-postgres)
	audit "logs-postgres"
	# Postgres is worth its own verb: on a cold start it runs initdb before either bot
	# can connect, and when that is what went wrong neither bot's log can say so.
	# Direct podman, not pod(): see the note on `logs-beacon` above.
	exec podman logs --tail "$TAIL_LINES" "$DB_CONTAINER" 9>&-
	;;
*)
	audit "REJECTED unknown-verb"
	usage
	;;
esac
